    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Fault slip distribution patterns derived from 3D seismic data from the Okan field in the Niger Delta have been analyzed. Log data, vertical seismic sections and a conceptual model for the growth and development of normal faults in the delta were used as aid in the analysis. The fault slip distribution patterns provide an additional utility in resolving fault geometry and in interpreting fault linkage and growth. The fault system under study appears to have developed as initially separate fault strands in the strike direction, each of which was variously segmented in the dip direction. The faults later linked in the strike direction to form one complex fault several kilometers in length. However, the vertical segmentation is still very complex, being determined by the relationship between fault throw and shale stratigraphy at any point in time.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
